UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 7, Lesson 3: The design process', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"A success criterion is a check you can try and mark yes or no, not a wish such as “looks good”. Jordan’s organiser has three criteria. It holds 8 pencils. It fits a desk corner of about 15 cm by 15 cm. It has no sharp edges. Each check needs an action: count the pencils after a gentle bump, measure the base, or run a fingertip along every edge. “Awesome” and “cool” cannot be checked, because people will disagree. Write the number, the size or the safety check in the criterion itself.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same technology/design idea to a new object or material, then compare two pieces of evidence for strength.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nRewrite these wishes as three criteria for Jordan: “holds enough pencils”, “not too big”, and “safe to touch”. Each rewrite must say what to count, measure or trace so another person could mark yes or no.\n\nFEEDBACK\nHolds eight pencils after one gentle bump, fits a 15 cm corner, and has no scratching edge. Enough pencils becomes a count of 8 that stay in after the bump. Not too big becomes a base that fits inside a corner of about 15 cm by 15 cm. Safe to touch becomes a fingertip test with no sharp point. Each line can be marked yes or no by someone who was not in the room when you wrote it.\n\nWATCH FOR\nUsing “looks cool” or “is awesome” as a success criterion for the pencil organiser. Those words cannot be counted, measured or traced, so two people can disagree forever. Replace each wish with a check that has a number, a measurement or a clear yes-or-no action.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nWrite three checkable criteria for a bookmark that must stay inside a library book. Use new checks: it marks one page, it does not tear the page, and it is shorter than the book. Do not reuse the eight-pencil count, the 15 cm desk corner, or the organiser edge test.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one observation, criterion or step you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Design and Technology","learning_intention":"A success criterion is a check you can try and mark yes or no, not a wish such as “looks good”. Jordan’s organiser has three criteria. It holds 8 pencils. It fits a desk corner of about 15 cm by 15 cm. It has no sharp edges. Each check needs an action: count the pencils after a gentle bump, measure the base, or run a fingertip along every edge. “Awesome” and “cool” cannot be checked, because people will disagree. Write the number, the size or the safety check in the criterion itself.","lesson_number":3,"lesson_role":"Learn","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: safe classroom tools/materials under teacher supervision","Device only when the lesson requires digital work"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["DIT5-EVL-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Write three checkable criteria for a bookmark that must stay inside a library book. Use new checks: it marks one page, it does not tear the page, and it is shorter than the book. Do not reuse the eight-pencil count, the  Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using criterion on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Using “looks cool” or “is awesome” as a success criterion for the pencil organiser.. Correction: Holds eight pencils after one gentle bump, fits a 15 cm corner, and has no scratching edge.. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Using “looks cool” or “is awesome” as a success criterion for the pencil organiser. Those words cannot be counted, measured or traced, so two people can disagree forever. Replace each wish with a check that has a number, a measurement or a clear yes-or-no action. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating criterion on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with criterion, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about criterion with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for criterion. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for criterion. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Ignore the observation or criteria and invent a more exciting result","Copy the model objects and call that independent work","Holds eight pencils after one gentle bump, fits a 15 cm corner, and has no scratching edge","Using “looks cool” or “is awesome” as a success criterion for the pencil organiser"],"question":"Rewrite these wishes as three criteria for Jordan: “holds enough pencils”, “not too big”, and “safe to touch”. Each rewrite must say what to count, measure or trace so another person could mark yes or no. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for criterion; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Careful use of check with clear evidence","Using “looks cool” or “is awesome” as a success criterion for the pencil organiser","A success criterion is a check you can try and mark yes or no, not a wish such as “looks good”","Holds eight pencils after one gentle bump, fits a 15 cm corner, and has no scratching edge"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with criterion?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named check, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Apply criterion to new material and name the check used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence","Using “looks cool” or “is awesome” as a success criterion for the pencil organiser","Name check only, without linking it to criterion"],"question":"Which response best applies criterion with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses design criteria, safe making steps or test evidence appropriately for this lesson on criterion. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Call the first attempt finished without testing","Using “looks cool” or “is awesome” as a success criterion for the pencil organiser","Holds eight pencils after one gentle bump, fits a 15 cm corner, and has no scratching edge","Start making with no criteria or safety check"],"question":"Which response best evaluates design criteria, safe making steps or test evidence when analysing criterion?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Start making with no criteria or safety check","Call the first attempt finished without testing","Using “looks cool” or “is awesome” as a success criterion for the pencil organiser","Name a criterion, safety step or test result and say how it improves criterion"],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about criterion?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea (A success criterion is a check you can try and mark yes or no, not a) with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Jordan’s three criteria can be marked yes or no: eight pencils, a 15 cm corner, and smooth edges","Using “looks cool” or “is awesome” as a success criterion for the pencil organiser","Start making with no criteria or safety check","Call the first attempt finished without testing"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about criterion?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster","Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue","Check criteria and safety, record a test result, and note one honest improvement"],"question":"Which action keeps design criteria, safe making steps or test evidence accurate for criterion?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Retype the worked example with no new example","Using “looks cool” or “is awesome” as a success criterion for the pencil organiser","Name check only, with no linked example","Write three checkable criteria for a bookmark that must stay inside a library book"],"question":"Which independent response best applies criterion to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about criterion with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating criterion on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using criterion on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for criterion. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with criterion, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Using “looks cool” or “is awesome” as a success criterion for the pencil organiser.. Correction: Holds eight pencils after one gentle bump, fits a 15 cm corner, and has no scratching edge.. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s5-design-technology-y9-w02-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Catalogue subject is Design and Technology. Proposed Year 7 alignment uses Design Innovation Technology 7–10 (2025) outcome DIT5-EVL-01, which the syllabus says may be adjusted for Stage 4 (DIT4-ADJ-01). The 2019 Design and Technology syllabus is in teach-out until 2029. Content-point mapping pending. Not NESA verified. Practical work is paper or cardboard only.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S4","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise criterion.","Read the mission before you start observing, designing or testing.","Keep the property, criterion or variable you are studying in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your evidence with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the observation, criterion or step that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore observations, criteria or fair tests.","Prefer accurate evidence over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your evidence and reason are both present.","Proofread labels, measurements and sequence of steps.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact observation, criterion or test step before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key observations or criteria.","Say what each observation or criterion shows about the idea.","A final label alone is weaker than recorded evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak claim with stronger evidence.","Change the claim or method, not only how neat the drawing looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh object or context that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original evidence and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new objects or data."]},"subject":"Design and Technology","success_criteria":["I can use criterion with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify check and explain why it matters.","I can practise bump independently on a new prompt.","I can notice edge needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"A success criterion is a check you can try and mark yes or no, not a wish such as “looks good”.","options":["A claim unrelated to the observation, test or criteria","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence","A success criterion is a check you can try and mark yes or no, not a wish such as “looks good”"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about criterion?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nJordan’s three criteria can be marked yes or no: eight pencils, a 15 cm corner, and smooth edges. Holds 8: eight pencils stay in when the desk gets one gentle sideways bump from a fingertip. Fits the corner: the base measures no more than 15 cm along each side of the corner space. No sharp edges: a fingertip traced around every edge finds no point that scratches skin. “It should look amazing” is not on the list, because nobody can agree how to mark it.\n\nTRY FIRST\nRewrite these wishes as three criteria for Jordan: “holds enough pencils”, “not too big”, and “safe to touch”. Each rewrite must say what to count, measure or trace so another person could mark yes or no.\n\nFEEDBACK\nHolds eight pencils after one gentle bump, fits a 15 cm corner, and has no scratching edge. Enough pencils becomes a count of 8 that stay in after the bump. Not too big becomes a base that fits inside a corner of about 15 cm by 15 cm. Safe to touch becomes a fingertip test with no sharp point. Each line can be marked yes or no by someone who was not in the room when you wrote it.","explain":"A success criterion is a check you can try and mark yes or no, not a wish such as “looks good”. Jordan’s organiser has three criteria. It holds 8 pencils. It fits a desk corner of about 15 cm by 15 cm. It has no sharp edges. Each check needs an action: count the pencils after a gentle bump, measure the base, or run a fingertip along every edge. “Awesome” and “cool” cannot be checked, because people will disagree. Write the number, the size or the safety check in the criterion itself.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen, measured or tested against criteria.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties, criteria or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer. For practical technology work, follow teacher safety rules for tools, food and devices.","icon":"📖","notice":"Using “looks cool” or “is awesome” as a success criterion for the pencil organiser. Those words cannot be counted, measured or traced, so two people can disagree forever. Replace each wish with a check that has a number, a measurement or a clear yes-or-no action. Correct the claim, criteria use or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Countable pencil, corner and edge checks beside wishes that cannot be marked.","caption":"A criterion needs a count, a measure or a yes-or-no action.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Can be checked</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">8 pencils; 15 cm; smooth</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Cannot be checked</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">Looks awesome or cool</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which observation or criterion matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the observations, criteria or test steps that carry the idea before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Jordan’s three criteria can be marked yes or no: eight pencils, a 15 cm corner, and smooth edges. Holds 8: eight pencils Then write your own evidence.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which observation or criterion matters most.\nJuno: Name the observations, criteria or test steps that carry the idea before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Jordan’s three criteria can be marked yes or no: eight pencils, a 15 cm corner, and smooth edges. Holds 8: eight pencils Then write your own evidence."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Make the technology idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim. Supported records follow the actual observations, criteria and purpose of the task. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A claim unrelated to the observation, test or criteria","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence","Make the technology idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about criterion?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nJordan’s three criteria can be marked yes or no: eight pencils, a 15 cm corner, and smooth edges. Holds 8: eight pencils stay in when the desk gets one gentle sideways bump from a fingertip. Fits the corner: the base measures no more than 15 cm along each side of the corner space. No sharp edges: a fingertip traced around every edge finds no point that scratches skin. “It should look amazing” is not on the list, because nobody can agree how to mark it. Look at each observation, source detail or example that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key observations, sources or steps from the model and say what each one shows.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label.","explain":"Make the technology idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen, measured or tested against criteria.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties, criteria or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer. For practical technology work, follow teacher safety rules for tools, food and devices.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without evidence does not show technology thinking. Correct the claim, criteria use or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Show the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"A gentle bump count beside a fingertip edge trace for Jordan’s organiser.","caption":"The check is the action you would actually try.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Bump count</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">How many of 8 stay in</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Edge trace</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">Fingertip finds no scratch</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific technology error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim. Supported records follow the actual observations, criteria and purpose of the task. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison","A claim unrelated to the observation, test or criteria","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about criterion?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nUsing “looks cool” or “is awesome” as a success criterion for the pencil organiser. Those words cannot be counted, measured or traced, so two people can disagree forever. Replace each wish with a check that has a number, a measurement or a clear yes-or-no action. Here is a more productive model: Jordan’s three criteria can be marked yes or no: eight pencils, a 15 cm corner, and smooth edges. Holds 8: eight pencils stay in when the desk gets one gentle sideways bump from a fingertip. Fits the corner: the base measures no more than 15 cm along each side of the corner space. No sharp edges: a fingertip traced around every edge finds no point that scratches skin. “It should look amazing” is not on the list, because nobody can agree how to mark it.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against claim fails, then write a corrected claim with evidence.\n\nFEEDBACK\nHolds eight pencils after one gentle bump, fits a 15 cm corner, and has no scratching edge. Enough pencils becomes a count of 8 that stay in after the bump. Not too big becomes a base that fits inside a corner of about 15 cm by 15 cm. Safe to touch becomes a fingertip test with no sharp point. Each line can be marked yes or no by someone who was not in the room when you wrote it.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific technology error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen, measured or tested against criteria.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties, criteria or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer. For practical technology work, follow teacher safety rules for tools, food and devices.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without repairing the evidence does not fix the error. Using “looks cool” or “is awesome” as a success criterion for the pencil organiser. Those words cannot be counted, measured or traced, so two people can disagree forever. Replace each wish with a check that has a number, a measurement or a clear yes-or-no action. Correct the claim, criteria use or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use the same technology idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence. Supported records follow the actual observations, criteria and purpose of the task. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A guess with no evidence","Use the same technology idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example","A claim unrelated to the observation, test or criteria","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about criterion?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA success criterion is a check you can try and mark yes or no, not a wish such as “looks good”. Jordan’s organiser has three criteria. It holds 8 pencils. It fits a desk corner of about 15 cm by 15 cm. It has no sharp edges. Each check needs an action: count the pencils after a gentle bump, measure the base, or run a fingertip along every edge. “Awesome” and “cool” cannot be checked, because people will disagree. Write the number, the size or the safety check in the criterion itself. Now choose a different example to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nInquire into or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short evidence-based reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same technology idea as the model, but contain different evidence.","explain":"Use the same technology idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen, measured or tested against criteria.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties, criteria or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer. For practical technology work, follow teacher safety rules for tools, food and devices.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new evidence. Correct the claim, criteria use or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Transfer to a new investigation"}],"title":"Year 7, Lesson 3: The design process","unit_number":1,"unit_title":"The design process","week":2,"week_in_unit":2,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nJordan’s three criteria can be marked yes or no: eight pencils, a 15 cm corner, and smooth edges. Holds 8: eight pencils stay in when the desk gets one gentle sideways bump from a fingertip. Fits the corner: the base measures no more than 15 cm along each side of the corner space. No sharp edges: a fingertip traced around every edge finds no point that scratches skin. “It should look amazing” is not on the list, because nobody can agree how to mark it.\n\nWrite what makes this example accurate, naming the observations, criteria, properties or test steps used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Countable pencil, corner and edge checks beside wishes that cannot be marked.","caption":"A criterion needs a count, a measure or a yes-or-no action.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Can be checked</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">8 pencils; 15 cm; smooth</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Cannot be checked</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">Looks awesome or cool</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"A gentle bump count beside a fingertip edge trace for Jordan’s organiser.","caption":"The check is the action you would actually try.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Bump count</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">How many of 8 stay in</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Edge trace</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">Fingertip finds no scratch</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 7"}') WHERE seed_key='s5-design-technology-y9-w02-l1' AND family_id IS NULL;
